3. Sole additional restriction: physical hardwiring of weights

Without separate written authorization from the Licensor, You may not
physically hardwire Covered Model or Covered Variant weights, in whole or
in a functional weight block, into a chip's non-reprogrammable physical
structure, or commission fabrication that does so.

"Physical hardwiring" means permanently encoding covered weight values or
equivalent representations of them in weight-specific circuit topology,
interconnects, mask-programmed ROM or fixed physical coefficients used for
model computation. Changing those encoded values requires refabrication
or alteration of the chip's physical structure, rather than writing model
data or reconfiguring programmable hardware.

A functional weight block is a set of weights that implements a model
operation or submodule. Generic mathematical constants, architecture
dimensions and operator settings alone are outside this restriction.
Quantizing, encoding, decomposing, reordering or splitting covered weights
does not exempt those weights if they remain physically hardwired as
defined above. Adding an updateable component does not change the status
of weights that remain physically hardwired.

4. Permitted hardware uses

Storing, loading, compiling, mapping and executing weights as model data
remain permitted, including on CPUs, GPUs, NPUs, programmable ASICs,
reconfigurable FPGAs, memory and rewritable compute-in-memory devices.
Preinstalling weights in a device, selling devices, and ordinary firmware
or storage distribution are permitted.

Read-only storage, non-volatility, write protection, offline operation,
a dedicated product purpose or a decision not to update a model does not
by itself constitute physical hardwiring. The names ROM, OTP, fuse or
antifuse do not alone determine whether the restriction applies; only
physical encoding of covered weights as defined in Section 3 does so.
Software, access-control or product-policy limits on updates are not
physical hardwiring. Calibration constants, security keys and device
identifiers that do not encode covered model weights are outside it.

Hardware design, compilation, simulation and supplying design files do
not themselves constitute physical hardwiring. This Supplement adds no
independent restriction on those activities or on distributing hardware.
The physical-hardwiring act described in Section 3 remains restricted.
Separately licensed software, independently trained models, architecture
ideas and generic operators remain outside this restriction. There are
no other additional field-of-use restrictions or royalties.

